artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider an ultra-dense wireless network modeled as follows. A large set of single-antenna (or few-antenna) access points (APs) is distributed in a service area and connected via a fronthaul network to one or more central processing units. A set of user equipments (UEs) is served jointly by the APs in a coherent, cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO) regime: for each UE k, a user-centric cluster C_k of nearby APs cooperatively transmits/receives signals so that the effective channel seen by k is the superposition of the channels from all APs in C_k. The system requires:

(i) accurate channel state information (CSI) at each AP in C_k, typically acquired via uplink pilots within a coherence block of length τ_c symbols and coherence bandwidth B_c;
(ii) phase/frequency synchronization and reciprocity calibration across all APs in C_k so that coherent joint transmission is possible;
(iii) scheduling of pilot and data resources across UEs sharing pilots (pilot contamination control);
(iv) routing of baseband samples and CSI over a capacity-limited fronthaul of finite latency.

Now suppose UEs move with velocities v inducing Doppler spreads f_D = v f_c / c (with carrier frequency f_c) that are comparable to or larger than 1/τ_c per coherence block, so that the user-centric clusters {C_k}, the CSI estimates, the synchronization/calibration parameters, the scheduling decisions, and the fronthaul routing must all be updated on a timescale shorter than the channel coherence time.

Design and analyze a CF-MMIMO operating scheme — specifying cluster formation rules C_k(t), pilot allocation and CSI acquisition protocol, synchronization/calibration update rule, scheduler, and fronthaul routing policy — that achieves a provable lower bound on per-user spectral efficiency that does not degrade to zero as the user mobility (Doppler) increases up to a specified range, while keeping control-plane signaling, fronthaul load, and computational complexity scalable in the number of APs and UEs.

In particular, determine whether such a scheme exists and, if so, characterize the achievable trade-off between maximum tolerable user velocity, network density (APs per unit area), per-UE spectral efficiency, and signaling/fronthaul overhead.
